  "decision": "D58 / spec r3 section 3: streets are a synthetic lexicon authored in this repository",
  "min_pool_size": 2000,
  "notes": [
    "Purpose: street-name VALUES for the benign street-address family (spec 2.1). A value is a street string only: it never carries a house number or a city. House numbers and cities are drawn independently at generator time and are never paired with a street here, so no 'number + street + city' of a real dwelling can be produced by this file.",
    "No real personal address and no real postal record is used. Stems are generic words, invented surnames or invented place-like words; a coincidence with a real street name somewhere is possible and harmless because no number or city accompanies it. Famous full person names are deliberately absent.",
    "Expansion is deterministic (see expand_streets in scripts/dataset/build_v5_benign_pools.py): language x stem x street-type form, then directional, unit, numbered-road, fixed-phrase and cross-street patterns. The pool is the de-duplicated set of strings, keyed by the expansion key.",
    "FINGERPRINT RISK: the pool comes from a small regular grammar (stem + type, optional directional / unit / cross street). A model could learn the grammar instead of the semantics 'this is a street'. Mitigations: several languages and abbreviation styles, apostrophes / hyphens / accents / ordinals / numbered roads / unit designators, and punctuation variety. Residual risk is NOT eliminated and is audited downstream (spec section 7, C-S checks); pool strings must not be the only way a street value can look.",
    "Skew risk: English dominates the pool (largest stem lists and the directional / unit / cross-street patterns apply to English only). Downstream sampling should not be proportional to pool size without a per-language cap.",
    "Unit designators contain '#', '.', digits and the ordinal signs; cross streets contain '&' and '/'. They are benign by construction and are kept on purpose (spec 2.1: legitimate punctuation in non-attack values).",
    "Flags in the built pool (has_apostrophe, has_hyphen, non_ascii, has_symbol) describe values and never filter them."
  ],
